vulkan: keep C++ exceptions inside the backend

ggml's Vulkan backend reports failures by throwing: vk::SystemError when a fence
or a submission fails ("device lost", "getFenceStatus"), std::runtime_error when
a device cannot be used ("does not support 16-bit storage"). Only backend
registration catches them. Everything else is reached through the C interface
(ggml_backend_dev_init, ggml_backend_graph_compute, ggml_backend_synchronize,
the tensor and buffer calls), and vibe-server calls that interface from Rust,
which cannot unwind a C++ exception: the process aborts with "fatal runtime
error: Rust cannot catch foreign exceptions". That was the last line of stderr
on 86 installs in two weeks of vibe telemetry (2026-10-31 to 11-14), nearly all
on Windows.

This patch:

- routes every interface entry that can throw through a guard that logs the
  exception and returns a failure the entry can express: GGML_STATUS_FAILED,
  a null backend or buffer, false;
- makes graph_compute wait for its own work. The backend submits in
  graph_compute and waits in synchronize, which returns nothing, so a lost
  device surfaced where it could not be reported. Waiting inside graph_compute
  turns it into a status. The synchronous compute functions synchronize right
  after it anyway, so this costs their callers nothing;
- stops ggml_vk_get_device from leaving a half-initialised device in its cache
  when set-up throws, so the next caller gets the same error instead of a
  device that was never finished;
- returns no backend from ggml_backend_vk_init when the device cannot be set
  up. Callers already take a null backend to mean "no GPU" and use the CPU.

Not upstream as of ggml v0.22.0. Applies after 0001-0004.

